and they're like changing in the bedroom and they're like, oh, that could sound like an

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.640
intruder as well as Michael Esposito from Nostalgia.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:07.900
Three intruders. Yeah.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:09.440
That's our band. Cool.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:12.940
So today we are talking about Rad Racer.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.920
Is Rad, like, is that still happening in the 80s?

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:17.820
Is Rad a cool bird?

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:19.180
Yeah, it's happening. No, I think so. Okay.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.440
All right. I'm just not sure if Rad was like 70s.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:24.240
No, it's more. If it was released today, it'd be on fleek racer.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:27.200
I would play on fleek racing.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:33.500
I think that would be awesome. So Rad Racer, that's by Square.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:37.400
And we all know that Square is going to go ahead and do Final Fantasy stuff, but they've

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.320
already done something for us on the NES.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:41.300
It came out just a month ago.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.800
Yeah. But for us, it was weeks and weeks ago.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.500
Yeah. And that was the 3D battles of World Runner.

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:51.540
Which was less than expected from Square.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:54.560
Like, you know, from the future expectations of Square.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:56.400
If you know what I mean.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:59.880
Yeah. And I think like in line with what the rest of the console was doing at the time.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:01.020
It was still behind.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:02.740
Yeah. Like, it still wasn't that great.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:07.540
It's like, wow, thanks for pretending to simulate 3D, but also you forgot the gameplay.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:14.920
Yes. And they tried to go about their 3D ideas in a different way with this one, didn't they?

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:18.660
I think so. I think for at least for this one, because the other one was following.

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:22.760
So something interesting about both these games for Square, in my opinion, is that they're

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:24.360
both copying Sega games.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:29.120
So we talked about like Space Harrier being the game that 3D World Runner was copying.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:30.580
And that was a huge hit at the arcade.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:34.800
But then also, Rad Racer is definitely copying OutRun.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.980
Sure. And OutRun is like the biggest thing ever.

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:42.300
I never played OutRun. OutRun is like one of the most successful arcade games of all time.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:43.320
It defines an aesthetic.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:46.060
I've seen the aesthetic, but I haven't actually played the game.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:47.520
Yeah, you should check that out.

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:49.300
Especially the arcade version.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:51.140
Only the arcade version.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.200
Well, I'm just saying, because that's like the one that everybody talks about.

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:57.280
I think it would be a little weird if you played like, I don't even know which Sega systems

00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:58.720
it got ported to. I think it's on Master System.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:02:59.760
Yeah, that would just be bizarre.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:00.600
It's not great.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:01.900
Okay. Yeah. Okay.

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:06.980
And so, with this time, even though Square does things a little different, we'll talk

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:10.800
about whether we like this game or not when we get into the gameplay thing.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.620
But I did want to mention one thing about Square, and that's this whole thing with like,

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:16.660
wow, another pseudo 3D game.

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:23.920
Why is that? And that's a big push from Square owner Masafumi Miyamoto, who wanted to demonstrate Nasir

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.260
Jebeli, who was the programmer.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:32.940
Yeah, Iranian programmer who Square acquired to do like a bunch of different games with

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:36.020
them. He wound up doing a bunch of Final Fantasy games with them too, but he's kind of like

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:41.360
this know-it-all programmer expert for those days where like, everybody was working in

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:45.080
assembly, which was a really hard program to get your head around.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:48.520
And he was just a whiz at it and knew how to do this special 3D programming.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:53.140
So they figured if they acquired him, they might as well, Miyamoto's thoughts was, I might

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:57.180
as well have him make a bunch of 3D-like games and let's see what we can do with it.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:04.820
But to wrap that up with the Famicom, there actually was a 3D system peripheral that only

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:10.140
for the Famicom that I can't find any details on, but I know that they did make 3D glasses.

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:13.820
But don't they just work like, you know, movie style 3D glasses?

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:17.420
Well, yeah, like the old style, like the red and blue style 3D.

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:23.420
But I couldn't find out if the peripheral was also something you needed to attach to the Famicom

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:27.120
to get the 3D mode to play out on the video side of it.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:30.740
But long story short, that doesn't come to the North American version, obviously.

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:37.560
But those glasses are now kind of like a collector's item because they made, they were made of paper

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:39.760
or plastic. So it's like...

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:43.820
But they did, because there is a 3D mode in the NES version too.

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:46.900
It's just they apparently didn't need a peripheral for it.

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:47.960
Well, there's no...

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:49.920
I'm sorry, you know, it doesn't pop out of the screen.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:52.700
Okay. Right? Like...

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:57.220
Sure. When I think of peripheral though, I'm thinking like a piece of hardware.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:02.920
Yeah. Right. Whereas this game, you can put on 3D glasses and it'll pop out of the screen, so to speak,

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:05.100
wouldn't it? Really? Yeah, you press select.

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:07.700
Okay. So it has that mode still in it.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:08.860
Okay. I never hit select.

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:11.960
I also didn't, but I read that in the manual.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:14.040
Okay. So this one made it over then.

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:18.360
Okay. Because I remember in 3D World Runner it didn't have that, but that's cool.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:23.140
I guess like any pair of red and blue glasses is going to work, but they never ship them

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:27.040
over to, you know, like Square never shipped over copies of the glasses.

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:30.440
So I guess it's just really lazy of them to be like, yeah, they don't need the glasses,

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:34.720
but we'll include the 3D mode in case they just have red and blue tinted paper lying

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:38.380
around. Hey man, I think this is the 80s that probably had some red and blue glasses.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:39.520
Yeah. We should get some.

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:40.660
I would like to try.

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:43.500
I'd like to see how bad the 3D pops out.

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:47.680
I mean, if you look at the game, even the 3D that it's trying to simulate, there's not

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:48.840
a lot on the screen.

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:54.840
Well, if we want to talk about the 3D effect, I think that as far as the 3D effect in NES

00:05:54.840 --> 00:06:00.600
games go, like so far, this is the most impressive that I've seen.

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:01.740
I think, yeah, I think it works.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:07.180
Like it actually has like rolling hills, sort of like you go over hills and it looks like

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:11.280
it. I think that also though, if we're, if we're getting into it, which it sounds like we're

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:15.880
getting into, uh, because of the 3D effect, it makes the signs really hard to read.

00:06:15.980 --> 00:06:17.920
Oh yeah. It's not always for the best.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:22.420
Yeah. So they kind of sacrifice, you know, and I think an important part of the gameplay there

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:26.500
where the arrows tell you which way you need to prepare to turn.

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:29.420
And this is a racing game.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:30.520
Yeah. It's a racing game.

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:35.280
Uh, so because of that, they're coming really fast at a distance.

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:37.780
So they start off really small and it looks great.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:41.580
It looks like a real sign, but you can't really read it because you're going well past the

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:44.540
speed limit. And I didn't mean to say that the 3D is lackluster.

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:49.160
I think what I meant is if you look at the game, it's really just kind of like a background,

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:51.620
a road and a car.

00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:55.780
So I'm wondering like, what's the need to have that kind of stuff pop out of your screen?

00:06:55.980 --> 00:07:01.280
Like it wouldn't be this, I would guess other cars maybe to see the other cars, like it

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:06.400
as they're in different, like as they're further away, seeing them be that's whatever in the

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:07.820
into the screen. That's what I would guess.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:10.860
I did that 3D back then also only ever came out of the screen.

00:07:11.020 --> 00:07:13.260
It didn't do like today's diorama style.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:17.720
Like, you know, if you want to go into the 3D, 3 and 3D, it looks like a toy box, you

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:22.880
know? Yeah. Whereas like all 3D back then just was like, oh my God, that dinosaur is going to eat my

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:24.920
face. Like, yeah.

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:30.200
So we'll get into the objective of the game at least, because I think that's kind of important

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:35.040
when we're talking about these racing games, because this one's not a race against other

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:37.560
cars. It's not a three lap system.

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:43.940
It's specifically a, like a rally course where you have to hit checkpoints in a certain amount

00:07:43.940 --> 00:07:47.680
of time. So you have a timer that usually is somewhere around like 70 seconds.

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:49.060
It counts down.

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:54.320
And if you get to the next checkpoint in time, you get to go again to the following checkpoint.

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:57.040
And then eventually you'll end the course and go to the next stage.

00:07:57.220 --> 00:07:59.140
I believe there's 10 stages?

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:00.480
Eight. Okay.

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:04.600
Have you read the object of the game description in the manual?

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:06.060
Go for it. It's a great story.

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:08.040
It's pretty good. I have it pulled up here.

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:13.160
As the last traces of color leave the sky, you're hanging out by your new wheels, anxious

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:14.220
for the race to start.

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:17.060
Transamerica. From the West Coast to the East Coast.

00:08:17.260 --> 00:08:18.400
Okay. It's time.

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:22.060
You jump into the driver's seat, feeling the awesome power beneath you.

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:27.120
Stepping through the gears, the cars around you fade into tiny specks in the rear view mirror.

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:29.760
Look out! You're on top of a hairpin curve.

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:31.980
Whoa. The tires screech.

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:33.460
I like that whoa. Whoa.

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:34.440
It doesn't even have like an exclamation point.

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:36.300
No exclamation point. Whoa.

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:41.980
The tires screech and your heart hits your ribcage, but you make it, laughing.

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:45.220
There's only a few more of these until you reach the planes.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:48.080
Then it happens. Another curve hits you in the face.

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:49.080
It's too fast.

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:52.760
You struggle for the shoulder, but the next thing you know, you're catapulting through the

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:56.340
air, almost weightless, until your body tears up against the seatbelt.

00:08:56.340 --> 00:08:57.980
Then, all is still.

00:08:58.320 --> 00:08:59.480
Not a scratch on you.

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:02.920
And the protective ceramic overcoating on your car did the trick.

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:04.060
No major damage.

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:06.340
You let out a scream.

00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:08.100
Turn the car around and hit it.

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:10.320
There's still time, but you've gotta be cool.

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:16.620
Alert. You're headed straight for the coast and those glorious checkered flags, defying the odds

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:18.520
again. Woo!

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:19.440
I mean, damn.

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:23.680
I mean, that's like Don Draper levels of, like, amp copy right there.

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:25.200
You just painted a picture for me.

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:32.320
Yeah. If I'm, like, doing marketing for this game, I buy commercial time, you know, around the

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:36.500
same time that, like, what was that show with the talking car?

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:49.760
I mean, Herbie wasn't not cool, it's just that I feel like Knight Rider was a little cool.

00:09:49.780 --> 00:09:51.180
Yeah, I don't know why I want to directly watch that.

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:56.160
You buy, like, 30 seconds of ad time during Knight Rider and you just show gameplay while

00:09:56.160 --> 00:09:59.980
you read what, like, whatever Sam just read, whatever it was in the manual, right?

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:03.460
Yeah, right. Like, you just read that and you're just like, ah, I'm sold on this game.

00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:05.700
Yeah. Yeah, I'd buy a whole system over that game.

00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:08.480
Then I'd play it and realize, oh, that's really overselling it.

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:12.340
Yeah. You know, there's no rear view mirror even.

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:14.600
They make crashing sound like it's a good thing.

00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:17.060
That's the bad thing. It's a good thing sometimes.

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:18.600
Crashing? I haven't read it.

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:20.000
Not in the game. Oh, okay, okay.

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:22.840
Well, in the game, it's, yeah, it's bad.

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:27.260
I think, like, they kind of expect crashing to be a part of the game because, to the point

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:33.640
that I think Sam was making earlier, you can't really tell when turns are about to happen

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:35.480
sometimes. Well, you could if you could read the damn signs.

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:38.800
No, yeah, but you can't because they're going by so fast because your car is going,

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:42.060
like, usually at least 170 kilometers an hour.

00:10:42.620 --> 00:10:45.940
So, when you're going that fast and then you automatically have to start making a turn,

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:47.660
you're either going to do one of two things.

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:52.240
You're either going to break completely, so this way you don't have to go jet over to

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:55.640
the sides, or you're going to jet over to the sides and hit, like, a tree or something

00:10:55.640 --> 00:11:02.440
like that. Or you could play this like an actual racing game and use, like, the apex turning mechanics

00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:05.400
that we all know and love because they work in this game.

00:11:05.560 --> 00:11:07.360
They say that in the manual.

00:11:07.380 --> 00:11:09.480
Yeah, it's in the manual, and I tried it out.

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:15.100
I tried to play it like I would like a more arcadey version of Gran Turismo, and I actually

00:11:15.100 --> 00:11:17.500
was doing pretty good. It felt good to play this game.

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:21.160
And I think that's a great point because one thing that I was doing after I, like,

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:25.300
crashed several times in a row was I think I was just trying to, like, beat the time

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:27.120
limit by, like, too much.

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:31.620
Like, if they give you 70 seconds, you're not supposed to, like, get to the checkpoint

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:33.180
with 20 seconds to go.

00:11:33.380 --> 00:11:34.340
You know? Oh, I did.

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:41.140
No, no, I'm saying in an ideal world, if you have 70 seconds, you should use all 70 seconds.

00:11:41.260 --> 00:11:46.580
So you should be trying to, like, you shouldn't be, it's not a race to get there as fast as

00:11:46.580 --> 00:11:49.640
you can. It's a race to make sure that you get to the checkpoint.

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:55.120
So I started slowing down and not trying to go, like, 200 kilometers an hour and actually

00:11:55.120 --> 00:11:56.720
adjust my speeds this way.

00:11:56.820 --> 00:12:01.960
If I did not see a turn coming, I could still brake and continue turning.

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:06.940
Mm-hmm. Because otherwise you're gonna, you know, we'll talk about other obstacles, but just

00:12:06.940 --> 00:12:11.360
your car going that fast alone makes it very hard to make any kind of turns when you're

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:14.340
trying to go 210, 220 kilometers an hour.

00:12:14.440 --> 00:12:20.640
Yeah. Well, aside from the difficulty with turning, did you guys feel that there was a sense of

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:21.620
speed with this game?

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:22.780
Yeah. I think so.

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:24.180
I felt a good sense of speed.

00:12:24.200 --> 00:12:29.600
I was actually surprised that you get to choose between a Ferrari and a Formula One car.

00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:31.640
Mm-hmm. Is the Formula One car faster?

00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:32.500
I don't think there's any difference.

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:36.000
Right. It seemed weird that it was just an aesthetic choice because...

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:37.180
Yeah. Because I'm always gonna pick the Ferrari.

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:39.740
I know. I was driving the Ferrari and I was like, this is awesome.

00:12:39.740 --> 00:12:44.160
I can't wait to drive the F1 car where things are gonna be like 300 kilometers an hour.

00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:46.460
But I feel like 255 was the max?

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:51.680
Yeah. That's like a, that's the highest integer that this system could create.

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:54.280
Right. Yeah. So they were like, cool, this is how fast you're going.

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:57.680
Mm-hmm. And I was just surprised by that, but I definitely feel fast.

00:12:57.680 --> 00:13:02.060
And I think that's because of how fast the trees and the signs go by.

00:13:02.060 --> 00:13:03.800
If you don't have anything else going by.

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:07.980
I think that's not, I think that's it. I think that's also the way that they programmed handling.

00:13:08.320 --> 00:13:15.080
Um, the fact that you can't just go from one side of the screen to the other, uh, at will

00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:20.580
also adds to that sense of speed because you feel, uh, your traction give way.

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:26.120
Yeah. Uh, it's, they also illustrate it with a kind of annoying, like, screeching sound for skidding

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:32.800
tracks, but, um, I think that with the, how the actual handling is designed as well as

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:36.800
the, how fast things are coming at you makes it all feel much faster.

00:13:37.300 --> 00:13:42.380
And I think it also helps that there's, uh, four dotted line, uh, lanes.

00:13:42.500 --> 00:13:44.300
Yeah. Through each, each, each thing.

00:13:44.380 --> 00:13:48.600
So this way, no matter where you are, you have at least, I think all four actually stay

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:50.000
on screen, no matter where you are on it.

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:54.140
So you have four of these things just racing by you, which would be cool in 3d, I suppose.

00:13:54.320 --> 00:13:57.740
Yeah. Uh, you know, having a, just kind of jut out at your eyes.

00:13:58.300 --> 00:14:02.480
But now I guess we should talk about the, uh, the, uh, the other obstacles, which are

00:14:02.480 --> 00:14:03.380
just the other cars.

00:14:03.900 --> 00:14:05.780
I found them to be incredibly annoying.

00:14:06.160 --> 00:14:07.180
Yeah. I don't know.

00:14:07.380 --> 00:14:12.800
I think that, that you need some kind of, you need some kind of obstacle to get around

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:14.200
this game to make it feel like anything.

00:14:14.460 --> 00:14:16.980
Absolutely. I'm not, I'm not saying that they're annoying because they're the obstacle.

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:21.460
I think they're annoying because it should work like bumper cars, but it, it works more

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:27.900
like they have the force of like 10,000 tons and you are just like a, a little, uh, balloon

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:29.120
next to them. I guess.

00:14:29.420 --> 00:14:34.500
Anytime I touched any of those cars, specifically even like from behind, I get like knocked!

00:14:35.120 --> 00:14:36.540
Yeah. Yeah. I'm going to go to the left or right.

00:14:36.700 --> 00:14:41.120
And I just feel like really like you couldn't just jump me over a little bit or make me

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:44.660
go like off course slightly like drift that way.

00:14:44.840 --> 00:14:46.860
I mean, sometimes when you hit them, that's all that happens.

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:53.260
Uh, I think that depending on how you hit them, it depends, it changes the way that you, your

00:14:53.260 --> 00:14:54.360
car reacts. Yeah.

00:14:54.580 --> 00:14:58.360
If they're going way slower than you, you're just going to get flipped off the road.

00:14:58.660 --> 00:15:03.520
But if they're going around the same, uh, around the same speed as you, then it'll be

00:15:03.520 --> 00:15:04.480
a more subtle difference.

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:06.420
But I, I think that's the way it is.

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:09.820
Hmm. Yeah. I think my problem was I just tried to go as fast as I could at all times.

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:15.580
Yeah. I guess I didn't really feel like it was that nuanced, but if that's the case, I think that's

00:15:15.580 --> 00:15:17.620
pretty cool that they programmed it along with speeds.

00:15:17.900 --> 00:15:23.640
I just kind of felt like it was touching these guys just sent me into not like it wasn't

00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:27.380
like I was instantly punished, but it just felt like, okay, I have to avoid these guys

00:15:27.380 --> 00:15:29.380
no matter what, but they can do whatever they want to me.

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:32.820
It doesn't seem fair that whenever you touch them, they don't move.

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:35.560
Yeah. They sort of stop rendering cars.

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:40.180
If you pass them, like there's never a car that ends up coming up from behind you.

00:15:40.380 --> 00:15:42.880
Yeah. So I guess they got around that.

00:15:43.020 --> 00:15:44.660
I don't know. Which is something Mach Rider did.

00:15:44.660 --> 00:15:46.640
Mach Rider could have cars come.

00:15:46.700 --> 00:15:47.900
Yeah. And you have a rear view.

00:15:48.300 --> 00:15:51.800
Yeah. They would come back in front of you.

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:59.720
Yeah. One thing I was a little bit bothered by was that there's in the manual, it says that if

00:15:59.720 --> 00:16:01.480
you, what is it?

00:16:01.520 --> 00:16:03.220
If you go behind another car, what's it like?

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:05.180
You can slipstream. I never noticed.

00:16:05.340 --> 00:16:08.280
Yeah. Because you're already going way faster than all those other cars.

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:12.840
Maybe in some of the later levels that I didn't really get to, that's more of a thing.

00:16:12.840 --> 00:16:18.100
Mm-hmm. But I feel like I'm never going to try, like slow down to get behind a car so that I can

00:16:18.100 --> 00:16:19.140
speed up to go around it.

00:16:19.260 --> 00:16:24.960
You know? I think that, yeah, definitely the strategy that you take with this game, if you're going

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:30.880
to try and, like how Mike said, just try and get to the end, you could, you'll be a

00:16:30.880 --> 00:16:33.780
little bit more tactical in how you approach.

00:16:34.440 --> 00:16:39.440
Like there'll be like three cars taking all three lanes and you have to figure out like

00:16:39.440 --> 00:16:41.120
which one's going to go forward first.

00:16:41.360 --> 00:16:42.420
Right. Try to get around them.

00:16:42.820 --> 00:16:48.060
Maybe there's some acceleration boosts if you're behind them for a while, but I didn't

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:48.940
notice anything either.

00:16:48.940 --> 00:16:54.180
I'm inclined to believe it though, just because there were some certain cars, even like in

00:16:54.180 --> 00:16:59.140
the relatively early courses, like three and four, there were some cars that I couldn't

00:16:59.140 --> 00:17:05.240
necessarily get around and I didn't know about this slipstream thing, but I was able to eventually

00:17:05.240 --> 00:17:08.760
like by coasting behind them, be able to get in front of them.

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:13.120
And that, you know, that's just probably, it's not, it doesn't have to do with like them

00:17:13.120 --> 00:17:16.100
moving over or me just accelerating in speed.

00:17:16.100 --> 00:17:18.220
I feel like it has to do with that slipstream mechanic.

00:17:18.400 --> 00:17:19.660
So I think, I think it works.

00:17:20.020 --> 00:17:25.920
I just, I think a lot of this stuff that is like communicated in the manual, there's, there's

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:27.680
not enough going on in the UI.

00:17:27.940 --> 00:17:33.620
Yeah. In this game, like, like there's just the time your score, which to me means nothing in this

00:17:33.620 --> 00:17:37.340
game since your score is how far, how many checkpoints you got, how far you got in the

00:17:37.340 --> 00:17:43.340
game. And then there's your speed and your speed is only like, your speed doesn't change when

00:17:43.340 --> 00:17:48.300
you're on like the snowy Alps course versus the, you know, like the speed is just a constant

00:17:48.300 --> 00:17:50.920
thing. And is that your RPMs in the top?

00:17:51.520 --> 00:17:55.880
Yeah. I don't think it has too much to do with anything.

00:17:56.140 --> 00:18:01.460
Yeah. It might have to do with, it might help with turns a little bit, but yeah, I don't know.

00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:04.900
I really didn't look at it because the only difference to me was like, I'm just always in the

00:18:04.900 --> 00:18:06.900
yellow and if I'm in the red, I'm probably going too fast.

00:18:06.900 --> 00:18:10.660
Mm-hmm. But, uh, I thought, you know, like those things are nice.

00:18:10.780 --> 00:18:11.740
I would have just liked to see more.

00:18:11.860 --> 00:18:15.180
Yeah. Like you mentioned Mach Rider had a rear view mirror and I guess you don't need it here

00:18:15.180 --> 00:18:19.680
because the cars never pop back up, but why not some more car like things to help?

00:18:19.740 --> 00:18:26.060
Yeah. Or even a visual cue with the slipstream or an audio cue, something just to let you know

00:18:26.060 --> 00:18:30.980
it's happening. And maybe this is just a complaint by it being like too arcadey, but I was kind of surprised

00:18:30.980 --> 00:18:32.100
that there was no gear system.

00:18:32.100 --> 00:18:32.940
Yeah.

00:18:33.700 --> 00:18:42.400
I, well, they do have, so Mach Rider didn't have, uh, the capability to go into turbo,

00:18:42.580 --> 00:18:43.460
did it? No.

00:18:43.860 --> 00:18:46.940
But I mean, fourth gear was pretty fast, so.

00:18:47.120 --> 00:18:50.740
Yeah. Um, but this game uses all of its inputs already.

00:18:50.960 --> 00:18:52.160
I don't know if they could.

00:18:52.160 --> 00:18:53.920
Yeah, but it uses one just for changing the radio station.

00:18:53.920 --> 00:18:55.060
But isn't that cool?

00:18:55.080 --> 00:18:56.880
I was going to like rave about that.

00:18:57.240 --> 00:18:58.080
Yeah. I think you should.

00:18:58.880 --> 00:19:00.060
I guess it's pretty cool, but.

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:06.480
It's not perfectly placed because I often accidentally changed the station just by mashing on the

00:19:06.480 --> 00:19:08.440
D-pad to try and like counter steer.

00:19:08.900 --> 00:19:13.360
But I think that because it's there, I, I just appreciate that.

00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:15.120
I think it goes along with the aesthetic a lot.

00:19:15.400 --> 00:19:18.680
I think even if you do it, even if you do it on accident though, it's nice because it's

00:19:18.680 --> 00:19:21.780
like, at least you're not listening to the same thing over, you know, like how many times

00:19:21.780 --> 00:19:25.640
we complain about games where it's like that one level just has that one song and you hear it the

00:19:25.640 --> 00:19:26.740
entire time. It's like.

00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:27.760
Or you can listen to nothing.

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:31.120
Yeah. That is a nice choice. And that is something that we would really like in a lot of games.

00:19:32.060 --> 00:19:33.000
No, that's fair.

00:19:33.140 --> 00:19:36.160
But, you know, at the sacrifice of less mechanics, I guess.

00:19:36.400 --> 00:19:41.800
But to be fair, also the, the, uh, the mechanics that are there do feel pretty good.

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:44.260
Yeah. You know, I think driving car feels good.

00:19:44.480 --> 00:19:45.400
I don't know how.

00:19:45.900 --> 00:19:49.700
It would definitely feel like a different game if there was actual gear shifting in this, like

00:19:49.700 --> 00:19:50.860
there was in mock rider.

00:19:51.160 --> 00:19:58.000
Yeah. Uh, I think you already have a lot to handle and a lot to manage with, uh, all of the,

00:19:58.100 --> 00:19:59.680
cause there's a lot more obstacles in this game.

00:19:59.860 --> 00:20:02.740
I feel at least moving obstacles, not so much.

00:20:02.920 --> 00:20:04.140
And you can't shoot any of them.

00:20:04.440 --> 00:20:06.280
You can't shoot them to get them out of the way.

00:20:06.400 --> 00:20:07.480
It's a grounded game.

00:20:07.580 --> 00:20:08.860
Yeah. It's grounded in reality.

00:20:10.160 --> 00:20:14.600
Um, but yeah, I think it would be a very busy game if it also had that.

00:20:14.660 --> 00:20:17.180
So you're not rooting for any more obstacles to be added.

00:20:17.180 --> 00:20:19.440
You're comfortable with the trees, the signs and the cars.

00:20:20.020 --> 00:20:27.960
Just asking. I mean, I think that if this game could find some way to put other things along the side

00:20:27.960 --> 00:20:29.060
of the road, that would be great.

00:20:29.180 --> 00:20:32.360
But I feel like that was a, that was probably a hardware issue.

00:20:33.020 --> 00:20:39.680
Um, because if you look at the ported versions of Outrun, none of them have cool stuff.

00:20:39.680 --> 00:20:46.800
And when they do try and put stuff on the side of the road, you're dropping like 20 frames per second there.

00:20:47.200 --> 00:20:53.080
And I think like, I maybe glossed over this at the top, but I think the backgrounds in this game are pretty awesome.

00:20:53.360 --> 00:20:56.380
They're great. Yeah. There's that one, um, the second level.

00:20:56.700 --> 00:20:59.800
Second level is great. The nice San Francisco city.

00:20:59.940 --> 00:21:01.160
I thought that was like so cool.

00:21:01.160 --> 00:21:12.900
I also thought that, um, like just color in this game is great for an NES game in the, I don't know if it's the last, uh, the last track of the second, last track.

00:21:12.900 --> 00:21:23.940
Uh, you are racing at dawn and the beginning of the, of the race, it's sort of, you know, that hazy, uh, twilight blue.

00:21:24.320 --> 00:21:30.420
And as you go farther, the sun rises and it changes, the sky changes colors.

00:21:30.620 --> 00:21:33.960
There's, there's more stuff that just looks like sunrise.

00:21:35.240 --> 00:21:36.700
I guess I'm having trouble.

00:21:36.940 --> 00:21:42.600
And you start to even see like, um, I think at certain points, if you're up, if you go up on the hill, you can actually see the waterfront.

00:21:42.600 --> 00:21:50.340
Like you can see like an ocean and it's like weird because then you dip back down, uh, down the hill and no longer can you see the ocean part.

00:21:50.500 --> 00:21:55.920
You see grass. So it's like, that's a neat touch as if you're actually like racing to a destination.

00:21:56.780 --> 00:21:58.400
So I, I think the backgrounds are awesome.

00:21:58.580 --> 00:22:03.440
I never really thought about the colors, but looking at the game now, I do think the colors are really nice for an NES game.

00:22:03.840 --> 00:22:05.740
There's actually like a gradient change too.

00:22:05.880 --> 00:22:09.880
It's not just here's one frame where it's dark blue and here's one frame where it's light blue.

00:22:09.880 --> 00:22:15.740
So yeah, there are certain games that I feel like try to use this same color palette and come off like cartoony.

00:22:15.940 --> 00:22:17.500
And this doesn't do that.

00:22:17.680 --> 00:22:25.720
Yeah. Um, one other thing before we, uh, this is going back to cars and obstacles.

00:22:25.720 --> 00:22:30.200
The one thing else, the other thing that I appreciated was that these are actual cars.

00:22:30.460 --> 00:22:34.500
Yeah. Like per track, like the night LA track has Corvettes on it.

00:22:34.840 --> 00:22:36.900
And, and another track later there's Lamborghinis.

00:22:38.060 --> 00:22:43.540
Um, you can, you only see the back, the back half of them, but they're pretty, uh, iconic.

00:22:43.960 --> 00:22:50.340
Yeah. And they tried to go for like where you were in the world too, which I thought was like a European car in Greece.

00:22:50.340 --> 00:22:52.920
And yeah, they had the Lotus in one of them.

00:22:53.120 --> 00:22:57.320
Yeah. So, uh, some, actually I'm going to, I'm going to talk one more thing.

00:22:57.540 --> 00:23:07.580
Uh, the, the timer mechanic, one thing that I found interesting about that is that there was one time where the time ran out for me, but I was going like, let's say 200 kilometers or whatever.

00:23:08.480 --> 00:23:13.580
And it doesn't like, as soon as time hits zero, it doesn't stop and say game over.

00:23:13.800 --> 00:23:15.300
Mm-hmm. You actually like coast.

00:23:15.500 --> 00:23:16.360
Yeah. In the car.

00:23:16.540 --> 00:23:18.040
And if you cross the checkpoint.

00:23:18.240 --> 00:23:19.700
Yeah. You get your time back.

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:21.960
Yeah. It's like, oh, cool.

00:23:22.120 --> 00:23:25.480
I think that, that also happened in, uh, mock writer.

00:23:25.780 --> 00:23:26.860
Oh, you get to coast for a little bit.

00:23:26.980 --> 00:23:27.920
I couldn't remember. Yeah.

00:23:28.500 --> 00:23:31.480
Um, I kind of want to go back and play mock writer now after playing this one just to compare

00:23:31.480 --> 00:23:32.320
the two. Yeah.

00:23:32.320 --> 00:23:36.380
I'm having similar thoughts because I'll, I'll get into it later comparing the two.

00:23:36.500 --> 00:23:42.260
Yeah. And then, uh, so this is my, like what I'm going to call unrealistic wish list for this

00:23:42.260 --> 00:23:47.140
game, but, and I'm not saying that this game was capable of these things, but just knowing

00:23:47.140 --> 00:23:50.420
like what I wanted from it as I was playing it, these are just some thoughts I had.

00:23:50.740 --> 00:23:55.120
So one, if they're going to do this whole 3d thing, I would have loved a mode that puts

00:23:55.120 --> 00:23:56.100
you in the driver head.

00:23:56.220 --> 00:24:00.220
So this way you actually see like the steering wheel and you have like, I feel like that could

00:24:00.220 --> 00:24:03.180
be done. Yeah. In this style of cartoony.

00:24:03.340 --> 00:24:04.440
Yeah. You're really just changing.

00:24:04.720 --> 00:24:08.880
You're just giving it a layer in front of it that doesn't move.

00:24:09.060 --> 00:24:10.020
Yeah. And it, yeah, exactly.

00:24:10.140 --> 00:24:12.700
It would have almost like obscured more of your view.

00:24:12.940 --> 00:24:17.640
Yeah. Right. And so I feel like that would have been a harder mode, but maybe like a more realistic

00:24:18.500 --> 00:24:20.640
simulation style for, for that kind of thing.

00:24:20.640 --> 00:24:23.800
And I think that would also, since there's only eight stages, it would encourage you to

00:24:23.800 --> 00:24:28.900
try them another way to be like, Oh, you know, it was one thing to see the whole city skyline,

00:24:28.900 --> 00:24:32.700
but now imagine if you can only see it from your, you know, the front view of the car.

00:24:32.700 --> 00:24:37.660
Yeah. I feel like it would, you know, it would take on a different, uh, type of 3d.

00:24:38.060 --> 00:24:38.920
Different perspective. Yeah.

00:24:39.760 --> 00:24:45.720
And another, uh, thing on my wishlist was just like a different mode other than this, uh,

00:24:45.860 --> 00:24:49.160
race against the cars where, where the cars are only the opticals.

00:24:49.240 --> 00:24:53.120
I would have liked something maybe where there was like even just two other cars that you

00:24:53.120 --> 00:24:58.160
race against, uh, like kind of AI and you have like a, you know, finish in first, finish

00:24:58.160 --> 00:25:00.780
in second and finish in third, and you can choose your course ahead of time.

00:25:00.780 --> 00:25:07.100
Yeah. You know, another thing is like a two player mode where you can play with like a, you know,

00:25:07.180 --> 00:25:12.780
like a split screen, uh, horizontal view for each person and you can race against each other.

00:25:13.000 --> 00:25:17.480
And you, you preface this by saying like maybe not realistic, not realistic.

00:25:17.840 --> 00:25:21.840
Yes, exactly. Just things that I thought would have put this game over the top for me.

00:25:22.040 --> 00:25:27.260
Mm hmm. Things that it was like, I mean, like for me, this game is, is pretty competent and, and sets

00:25:27.260 --> 00:25:28.740
out what it wants to do.

00:25:28.740 --> 00:25:32.860
I, I will still say that like, even though we said there is a way to work out the turns,

00:25:33.060 --> 00:25:37.800
I still think the turns are a problem because I couldn't always tell when that was going

00:25:37.800 --> 00:25:39.620
on. And I don't necessarily think that that's a player.

00:25:40.260 --> 00:25:42.920
Yeah. It's more or less as a design flaw.

00:25:43.160 --> 00:25:46.300
A lot of the times. It does say in the manual to memorize the routes.

00:25:46.700 --> 00:25:47.880
So that's a joke.

00:25:47.880 --> 00:25:51.620
That was a joke. I was going to make to you early on was like, Oh, you didn't study now.

00:25:51.860 --> 00:25:53.760
It's 2d outline of the map beforehand.

00:25:53.760 --> 00:25:55.380
It tells you to, to memorize.

00:25:55.520 --> 00:25:58.860
I think that's a little different than memorizing like where you are in Metro.

00:25:58.920 --> 00:26:03.000
I agree. And I've been pretty high in this game, but I do agree that there should be a little

00:26:03.000 --> 00:26:04.100
bit more telegraphing.

00:26:04.200 --> 00:26:06.600
Yeah. How to go about those turns.

00:26:06.720 --> 00:26:07.640
But we, what were you going to say?

00:26:07.740 --> 00:26:09.740
I'm wondering if that's just them covering their bases.

00:26:10.040 --> 00:26:13.440
Maybe. Yeah. Another thing to nitpick.

00:26:13.540 --> 00:26:16.280
Maybe this isn't a nitpick, but this is actually a genuine problem.

00:26:17.600 --> 00:26:21.900
As good as I think that this game looks, and I do think that it looks good, even though

00:26:21.900 --> 00:26:23.540
the sides of the roads are pretty bland.

00:26:25.300 --> 00:26:26.700
There's a lot of flicker.

00:26:27.600 --> 00:26:28.840
Did you guys notice this?

00:26:29.160 --> 00:26:31.700
It was kind of hard. I think this contributes a lot.

00:26:31.820 --> 00:26:36.880
Maybe it wouldn't be so much of a problem to get around turns and to see when the obstacles

00:26:36.880 --> 00:26:40.320
were coming at you from a distance if there just wasn't so much flicker.

00:26:40.600 --> 00:26:43.820
And it's mostly a hardware limitation thing.

00:26:43.940 --> 00:26:50.040
Yeah. But even the cars, as they get like really close to the screen, they'll like lose like

00:26:50.640 --> 00:26:51.660
half of the car.

00:26:51.660 --> 00:26:54.320
Half of the car. It's like, you know, it's still there.

00:26:54.460 --> 00:26:56.480
Like you can fill in the gap with your brain.

00:26:56.620 --> 00:26:59.020
But it is kind of like, it's weird to see.

00:26:59.320 --> 00:27:00.300
Yeah, no, it's as I'm saying. Because.

00:27:00.500 --> 00:27:02.620
Sprite limit thing. You know how it flickers when it.

00:27:02.760 --> 00:27:06.860
Yeah. And I think like, you know, that's easily solved nowadays with an emulator.

00:27:07.020 --> 00:27:09.520
You can actually turn on like unlimited sprites.

00:27:09.720 --> 00:27:10.900
Oh, it won't do that.

00:27:11.200 --> 00:27:16.140
But, you know, obviously at the time, I think if the, if this whole thing is like driving

00:27:16.140 --> 00:27:20.820
3D like never before and see all this thing, it's like the flickering thing and like cars

00:27:20.820 --> 00:27:24.340
half disappearing. It can't do well with like 3D mode.

00:27:24.540 --> 00:27:28.440
If, if you have sprites flickering and you're in that there and you're seeing and come at

00:27:28.440 --> 00:27:30.180
